<root main_tree_to_execute = "MainTree">

  <!--
    RECONSTRUCTED 2026-07-15. The 2026-07-15 accidental full-tree revert (see
    memory/project_bulk_revert_regression_2026_07_15.md) overwrote this file back to the
    2-branch Task_Empty placeholder template, wiring the entire BT tactics expansion
    (Batches A through D, 20 new Task_/Decorator_/Service_ nodes) out of the active tree,
    even though every Task_*.cpp source file survived untouched on disk.

    This is NOT a guess: it is a direct transcription of the tree sketch and per-node
    trigger table in the original design plan
    (~/.claude/plans/i-have-aerial-combat-bt-guide-detailed-m-serene-duckling.md, outside
    the project directory so the revert never touched it), cross-checked against every
    Task_*.cpp/DECO_*.cpp file's own providedPorts() and design-intent comments, which
    independently reference this same Gate 0 to 4 structure by name; for example
    Task_FlatScissors.cpp says: "Gate 2's shared Sequence (Rule_forTraining.xml) already
    confirmed close range and mutual off-boresight... before this node is ever ticked".

    One confirmed deviation from the plan's original sketch, applied here: Task_SingleSideOffset
    is wired as a BARE Fallback child (no wrapping Sequence/DECO_DistanceCheck), per its own
    cpp comment: a Range 3000 to 5000 XML gate would cut the node off mid-maneuver exactly
    when Distance drops below 3000m during its own "close" phase, which needs to keep
    running down to the 2000m handoff. It self-gates its 3000 to 5000m entry internally on a
    fresh claim instead, the same idiom Task_HighYoYoUp/Task_LowYoYo/Task_Evade already use.

    Gate 0 through 4 naming below matches the plan and the C++ comments exactly, so future
    edits can cross-reference either source directly.

    NOT verified against the actual compiled AIP_BASE.dll/AIP_BASE_target.dll: this reconstruction
    was validated via BehaviorTreeFactory tree construction only (bt_factory registration plus XML
    parse, which catches unknown-node/unknown-port errors), not a live flight test. Confirm with
    run_local_dogfight.py ownship-backend bt target-backend bt save-log (the plan's own
    verification step 3) before trusting this in training or submission.
  -->

  <!--
    F25 FIX APPLIED 2026-08-13: name= REMOVED from all 17 control nodes in this file.
    THIS TURNS THE TACTICAL LAYER ON FOR THE FIRST TIME. See COMPETITION_PLAN.md 4.1 F25.

    ROOT CAUSE. BehaviorTree.CPP builds a control node (Sequence/Fallback) with ZERO
    CHILDREN whenever it carries a name= attribute. Measured at build time via
    childrenCount(): all 3 anonymous composites had children (1, 8, 9); all 17 NAMED ones
    had 0. Confirmed causally - deleting only the name attribute from
    Gate2p5_GunSolutionHold's Sequence, changing nothing else, gave it its 4 children back.

    WHAT THAT MEANT. An empty Sequence returns SUCCESS immediately and an empty Fallback
    returns FAILURE immediately, so Gate2p5_GunSolutionHold (Sequence, 0 children) SUCCEEDED
    UNCONDITIONALLY AND WON EVERY TICK, blocking Gates 1/2/3/4 and the tail beneath it, while
    every other gate block failed or succeeded vacuously. The only nodes that had ever
    executed in this project's history were Gate0_ClimbToSafeAltitude and
    Tail_SingleSideOffset - the two bare leaves directly under the anonymous outer Fallback.
    All 23 Task_* maneuver classes, all five gate blocks and every decorator had NEVER RUN.
    That is the mechanical explanation for C2, A4 and every positional null in the register.

    SCOPE OF THIS EDIT. Only the 17 <Sequence>/<Fallback> name= attributes were removed
    (12 Sequence + 5 Fallback). Leaf Task_*/DECO_* names are UNTOUCHED - those nodes work,
    and their names are what GateTrace.h reports. Verified post-edit: 0 named control nodes
    remain, 23 Task_ and 29 DECO_ leaf names intact. No DLL rebuild required, XML only.

    CONSEQUENCE FOR READING TRACES. Control nodes no longer carry a name, so GateTrace.h
    will report them by node type rather than by their old Gate*_ labels. The Gate*_ names
    survive only in the comments below and in the docs. Re-labelling them for trace
    readability is a SEPARATE question - do not simply put name= back, that is the bug.

    RE-BASELINE REQUIRED. This is not a tuning change. Every BT-relative measurement taken
    anywhere in this project before this date is VOID, not merely suspect - they all describe
    a tree running two leaf tasks. Re-measure from scratch before trusting any prior number.

    REVERT: restore Rule_forTraining.xml.bak_pre_f25_20260813 /
    Rule_real_eagle.xml.bak_pre_f25_20260813 (both taken immediately before this edit), or
    git checkout the two rule XMLs.
  -->

  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <Sequence>

        <!-- EF 부분 트리-->
        <Sequence>
          <SelectTarget name="SelectTarget" BB="{BB}"/>
          <DirectionVectorUpdate name="DirectionVectorUpdate" BB="{BB}"/>
          <DistanceUpdate name="DistanceUpdateService" BB="{BB}"/>
          <CheckSight name="CheckSight" BB="{BB}"/>
          <AngleOffUpdate name="AngleOffUpdate" BB="{BB}"/>
          <AspectAngleUpdate name="AspectAngleUpdate" BB="{BB}"/>
          <EnergyStateUpdate name="EnergyStateUpdate" BB="{BB}"/>

          <Fallback>

            <!-- GATE 0 - Survival. Absolute top priority, untouched from the original 8-node tree. -->
            <Task_ClimbToSafeAltitude name="Gate0_ClimbToSafeAltitude" BB="{BB}"/>

            <!-- GATE 2.5 - Gun-solution hold. MOVED HERE 2026-08-06 (was between Gate 2 and
                 Gate 3). Kept the "2.5" name so the diff against the old tree stays readable.

                 WHY THE MOVE. The old placement's own comment said it "sits below Gate 1
                 (threat) and Gate 2 (merge) so survival and the merge still preempt". Measured
                 2026-08-06, that preemption is not occasional; it is total:

                   * BT vs BT and BT vs a straight-and-level autopilot target, N=20 each, full
                     200 s: ZERO WEZ contact and zero damage in either direction. 80 episodes
                     across three configurations, nothing scored.
                   * A Python mirror of StickController::GetStick fed with the BT's REAL VP
                     (agreement 0.997 correlation vs the actual RollCMD) shows that during close
                     tracking the commanded aimpoint sits 83-87 deg AWAY from the target, and
                     Roll_Effect = 0.0000, so PitchCMD, which is a PRODUCT with Roll_Effect,
                     is identically zero exactly when we are trying to track.
                   * An 85-deg-off aimpoint is the signature of the ownship-relative nodes:
                     Task_Notch writes MyLocation + perp*3000 (exactly perpendicular),
                     Task_Evade / Task_EnergyTactics / Task_JinkingTurn write
                     MyLocation + awayFromTarget. Those are Gate 1 and Gate 2 nodes.

                 So the tree reaches gun geometry (range is achieved ~3 % of steps, min ATA
                 4.4 deg) and then hands the tick to a defensive node before Gate 2.5 is ever
                 evaluated. Gate 2.5 is not failing its own decorators; it is not being reached.

                 The reference BT architecture in the AI Combat SDK guide orders its selector
                 HardDeck -> GunEngagement -> ThreatResponse -> offense, i.e. the SHOT above the
                 THREAT reaction, and states the principle directly ("offense beats defense;
                 BreakTurn only when necessary", "removing unnecessary defensive branches made
                 the tree stronger"). That guide is a DIFFERENT competition with a 12 deg WEZ
                 cone, so its thresholds must NOT be copied here where the cone is 1 deg, but
                 the ordering principle applies, and a 1 deg cone makes it matter more, not
                 less: we can least afford a defensive branch spending our tracking ticks.

                 Survival (Gate 0) still preempts everything, which is the one ordering the
                 reference also keeps absolute.

                 REVERT: move this whole Sequence back between Gate 2 and Gate 3 (a marker
                 comment is left at the old location), or restore
                 Rule_forTraining.xml.bak_gateorder_20260806.
                 UNVALIDATED at the time of writing; the BT vs autopilot baseline (0/20 WEZ
                 contact) is the control to re-run against. -->
            <Sequence>
              <DECO_DistanceCheck name="Gun_DistGt152p4" UpDown="Greater" Distance="152.4" BB="{BB}"/>
              <DECO_DistanceCheck name="Gun_DistLt914" UpDown="Less" Distance="914" BB="{BB}"/>
              <!-- WIDENED 5 -> 8 deg (2026-08-05): pseudo-hysteresis dwell, no rebuild. True
                   enter/exit hysteresis needs a RUNNING latch (SyncActionNode forbids RUNNING ->
                   C++ change); widening the single hold gate is the rebuild-free approximation,
                   holds the track through a defender's jink out to 8 deg instead of dropping at
                   5, giving the pure-pursuit loop more dwell to grind ATA toward the 1.0-deg WEZ.
                   Shares 8 deg with Gate 1's carve-out (complementary). Range stays 150-914 m
                   (physical gun range; WEZ max 914.4 m). Revert = 8 back to 5. -->
              <DECO_LOSCheck name="Gun_OwnATA_Lt8" UpDown="Less" InputLOS="8" Which="Own" BB="{BB}"/>
              <Task_GunTrack name="Gun_Track" BB="{BB}"/>
            </Sequence>

            <!-- GATE 1 - Immediate threat reaction. Each node self-gates its own trigger
                 (EnemyInSight_Target / Distance / Los_Degree_Target) internally on a fresh claim;
                 no XML-level decorators here by design (plan §1). Notch (narrowest: near-beam)
                 is checked before JinkingTurn (general close-range spoil) before Evade/TheBreak
                 (widest: any close threat).

                 FIX 2026-07-16 (real competition scenario audit): JinkingTurn's and Evade's C++
                 gates are EnemyInSight_Target (~191deg hemisphere, no range limit) + Distance
                 alone; neither excludes a symmetric, neutral head-on merge (HCA>=150, both
                 sides equally approaching, neither side actually threatened yet). At the real
                 Prelims starting range (2000-3000ft = ~610-914m), both conditions were trivially
                 true on BOTH aircraft, so Gate 1 fired a defensive jink/break for BOTH sides on
                 literally every match's opening merge, and Gate 2's purpose-built
                 Task_NoseToNoseTurn (below) never got a chance to run, because Gate 1 is checked
                 first and neither node's gate has any angular awareness. Wrapped both in a
                 same-threshold (150deg) HCA exclusion so a genuine near-head-on merge defers to
                 Gate 2 at any range, while non-head-on close threats (the actual intended use
                 case for both nodes) are unaffected. Verified this doesn't change Gate 1's
                 outcome for OBFM-defensive (HCA~0, chase geometry) or HABFM (Notch fires first
                 regardless, ~90deg beam angle never reaches these two nodes). -->
            <Fallback>
              <!-- HCA CARVE-OUT (2026-08-11). Task_Notch had NO angular guard, unlike its two
                   siblings below, which were given HCA less-than-150 exclusions on 2026-08-05 for
                   exactly this reason. Notch is FIRST in this Fallback and returns SUCCESS
                   whenever the enemy is within 2000 m at 70 to 110 deg aspect (Task_Notch.cpp),
                   which is precisely the official beam merge (610 to 914 m, ~90 deg). So it fired
                   on the spawn tick, Gate 1 succeeded, and the outer Fallback NEVER REACHED
                   GATE 2, shadowing Gate2_BeamMerge, Gate2_HeadOnMerge, Gate2_OffCenterMerge and
                   Gate2_NeutralFight alike. That is why C6 re-merge widening and the beam branch
                   both measured null. Same 150 deg threshold as the siblings, so a genuine
                   near-head-on merge (HCA 180 at the match spawn) now defers to Gate 2, while
                   non-head-on close threats, Notch actual intended use, are unaffected.
                   XML only, no rebuild. Revert: delete this Sequence wrapper and restore the bare
                   Task_Notch line. See COMPETITION_PLAN.md 4.1 F17. -->
              <Sequence>
                <DECO_AngleOffCheck name="Gate1_Notch_HCA_Lt150" UpDown="Less" InputAO="150" BB="{BB}"/>
                <Task_Notch name="Gate1_Notch" BB="{BB}"/>
              </Sequence>
              <!-- DEFENSIVE PRIORITY FIX (2026-08-13, XML-only, no rebuild). The Break was MOVED
                   ABOVE the jink and gated on an actual gun solution being on US. Read this before
                   reordering these two back.

                   MEASURED PROBLEM. Post-F25, with the tactical layer running for the first time,
                   obfm_defensive scores 1W/0D/29L with 16 deaths and 27.51 damage taken (was
                   0W/30D/0L and 0.00 damage taken purely because the pre-F25 opponent could not
                   shoot). A gate trace of that geometry
                   (artifacts/eval/postf25_obfmdef_gatetrace.*) shows the engagement is completely
                   locked: the attacker runs Gun_Track on 300/300 traced ticks (a continuously held
                   gun solution at 557 m, their ATA 4.7 deg), and we run Gate1_JinkingTurn on
                   300/300 ticks with NOTHING ELSE EVER WINNING. Task_Evade never executes.

                   WHY. In this Fallback the order was Notch -> JinkingTurn -> TheBreak, and
                   JinkingTurn's guards (HCA < 150, own ATA >= 8) are both trivially true in a
                   chase (HCA ~0, own ATA ~175), so it succeeds every tick and STARVES TheBreak.
                   We answered a tracking gun with an undifferentiated continuous jink, forever.

                   THE FIX. TheBreak now sits first and carries two extra conditions describing the
                   situation a break actually answers: the bandit is pointed at us (target ATA
                   < 20) and inside gun range (< 914 m, the WEZ max). Outside that, it fails and the
                   jink below still runs exactly as before, so non-gun close threats are unaffected.
                   Doctrine agrees: Jink/Reversal/Ditch/Radius Defense are responses to a SPECIFIC
                   weapon employment (COMPETITION_PLAN.md 4.1 section G), which is why F14's
                   UNCONDITIONAL break measured harmful. Note also BFM_MANEUVER_GAP_REFERENCE.md:
                   the vertical-reversal family (Split-S, sliceback, pitchback) that genuinely
                   defeats a tracking gun has NO node in this tree at all, so this fix is the best
                   available answer with existing nodes, not the doctrinally complete one.

                   REVERT: swap these two Sequences back and delete the two added decorators
                   (Gate1_Break_TheyArePointed_TgtATA_Lt20, Gate1_Break_InGunRange_DistLt914), or
                   restore Rule_forTraining.xml.bak_pre_gate1reorder_20260813. -->
              <Sequence>
                <DECO_AngleOffCheck name="Gate1_Break_HCA_Lt150" UpDown="Less" InputAO="150" BB="{BB}"/>
                <!-- Same gun-solution carve-out as Gate1_JinkingTurn below (own ATA >= 8): if WE
                     have the shot, do not defend. -->
                <DECO_LOSCheck name="Gate1_Break_NotGunSolution_OwnATA_Ge8" UpDown="Greater" InputLOS="8" Which="Own" BB="{BB}"/>
                <!-- ADDED 2026-08-13: only break when THEY are pointed at us and in gun range. -->
                <DECO_LOSCheck name="Gate1_Break_TheyArePointed_TgtATA_Lt20" UpDown="Less" InputLOS="20" Which="Target" BB="{BB}"/>
                <DECO_DistanceCheck name="Gate1_Break_InGunRange_DistLt914" UpDown="Less" Distance="914" BB="{BB}"/>
                <Task_Evade name="Gate1_TheBreak" BB="{BB}"/>
              </Sequence>
              <Sequence>
                <DECO_AngleOffCheck name="Gate1_Jink_HCA_Lt150" UpDown="Less" InputAO="150" BB="{BB}"/>
                <!-- GUN-SOLUTION CARVE-OUT (2026-08-05, XML-only, NO rebuild). Root-cause trace
                     found Gate 1 (this jink/break) sits ABOVE Gate 2.5 (Gun_Track) with no
                     angular awareness, so the instant a maneuvering defender's nose sweeps into
                     our forward hemisphere it yanks the nose off an in-hand shot -> 0 WEZ in 80
                     eval eps. This DECO_LOSCheck fires the jink ONLY when own ATA >= 8 deg; when
                     own ATA < 8 (we are tracking a gun solution) it FAILS, so Gate 1 falls
                     through and Gate 2.5 gets to hold. Shares the 8-deg threshold with the
                     widened Gun_OwnATA_Lt8 below (complementary: jink >= 8, gun-hold <= 8).
                     Deliberately aggressive: in a mutual high-aspect pass we keep OUR tracking
                     shot instead of defending; the failure mode was being too passive. Tune 8
                     against the geometry trace; revert = delete this one DECO_LOSCheck. -->
                <DECO_LOSCheck name="Gate1_Jink_NotGunSolution_OwnATA_Ge8" UpDown="Greater" InputLOS="8" Which="Own" BB="{BB}"/>
                <Task_JinkingTurn name="Gate1_JinkingTurn" BB="{BB}"/>
              </Sequence>
            </Fallback>

            <!-- GATE 2 - Merge & neutral-fight detection. -->
            <Fallback>

              <!-- Head-on merge: HCA>150, both ATA<20, Distance 500-10000m -> covers both the
                   Prelims opening merge (2000-3000ft = ~610-914m) and the Finals tie-break
                   (10,000+ft = ~3048m). Floor LOWERED from 3000 to 500 on 2026-07-16 (real
                   competition scenario audit): the original 3000m floor excluded the entire
                   Prelims starting range, so this node could only ever fire for the rare Finals
                   tie-break, never for a normal match's opening merge. 500m keeps a token floor
                   (this Sequence is for the OPENING merge specifically, not any incidental
                   close-range head-on alignment later in an already-established fight) while
                   safely covering the real ~610m minimum. See Gate 1's comment above for the
                   paired fix that was required for this to actually be reachable. -->
              <!--; real_eagle 2026-08-06: BEAM MERGE branch ; ADOPTED on mechanism, neutral on
                   both available benchmarks. Read this before removing it.
                   PROBLEM IT FIXES (traced): the official match starts antiparallel and abeam at
                   2000-3000 ft and NO Gate 0-2.5 branch matched that geometry ; Gate1 needs
                   HCA < 150 (actual 180), Gate2_HeadOnMerge needs BOTH ATAs < 20 (actual ~91),
                   Gate2p5 needs ATA < 8, Gate2_OffCenterMerge needs 3000-5000 m (actual 610). The
                   tree fell through to its lowest-priority default, which saturates roll toward an
                   aimpoint 86 deg off the target and 118 deg off its own nose: a max-rate turn
                   AWAY. Both aircraft mirrored it to within 0.3 deg, so range grew 610 m ; 3278 m
                   in 11 s and the match became a 2841 m orbit that timed out 0-0.
                   MEASURED EFFECT, BT-only both sides, same seed, first 10 s:
                       shipped        609.8 ; 3084.8 m,  ATA 91.3 ; 118.0 deg  (separating)
                       this branch    609.8 ; 2298.2 m,  ATA 91.3 ;  79.2 deg  (closing)
                   i.e. it turns toward the bandit instead of away, which is the behaviour the
                   tree was missing at the one geometry every prelim round starts from.
                   WHY IT IS ADOPTED WITHOUT A WIN-RATE GAIN. Neither benchmark can show one:
                   vs our own BT is saturated (73.3%, 0 losses ; nothing left to convert), and
                   self-play is symmetric (both aircraft read THIS file, so the edge cancels ;
                   measured 7W/15D/8L vs 7W/16D/7L baseline, unchanged). The only case where a
                   better merge pays is an opponent that does NOT have it ; i.e. the actual
                   competition ; and that is exactly what we cannot test, because the rule XML is
                   global to both DLLs. Adopted on the mechanism, which is measured, rather than
                   on a win rate, which is unmeasurable here.
                   Maneuver chosen by A/B (N=30 each, vs BT): NoseToTail 22/30, LeadTurn 22/30,
                   OneCircle 21/30, NoseToNose 21/30 ; all within noise, NoseToTail kept because
                   it produced the cleanest ATA reversal in the trace above.
                   REVERT: delete this Sequence. -->
              <Sequence>
                <DECO_AngleOffCheck name="Gate2_Beam_HCA_Gt120" UpDown="Greater" InputAO="120" BB="{BB}"/>
                <DECO_LOSCheck name="Gate2_Beam_OwnATA_Gt45" UpDown="Greater" InputLOS="45" Which="Own" BB="{BB}"/>
                <DECO_LOSCheck name="Gate2_Beam_OwnATA_Lt150" UpDown="Less" InputLOS="150" Which="Own" BB="{BB}"/>
                <DECO_DistanceCheck name="Gate2_Beam_DistLt2500" UpDown="Less" Distance="2500" BB="{BB}"/>
                <Task_NoseToTailTurn name="Gate2_Beam_NoseToTail" BB="{BB}"/>
              </Sequence>
              <Sequence>
                <DECO_AngleOffCheck name="Gate2_HCA_Gt150" UpDown="Greater" InputAO="150" BB="{BB}"/>
                <DECO_LOSCheck name="Gate2_OwnATA_Lt20" UpDown="Less" InputLOS="20" Which="Own" BB="{BB}"/>
                <DECO_LOSCheck name="Gate2_TgtATA_Lt20" UpDown="Less" InputLOS="20" Which="Target" BB="{BB}"/>
                <DECO_DistanceCheck name="Gate2_HeadOn_DistGt500" UpDown="Greater" Distance="500" BB="{BB}"/>
                <DECO_DistanceCheck name="Gate2_HeadOn_DistLt10000" UpDown="Less" Distance="10000" BB="{BB}"/>
                <Task_NoseToNoseTurn name="Gate2_NoseToNoseTurn" BB="{BB}"/>
              </Sequence>

              <!-- Off-center merge / re-merge: AA 30-90, Distance 3000-5000m, closing. -->
              <Sequence>
                <DECO_AspectAngleCheck name="Gate2_AA_Gt30" UpDown="Greater" AA="30" BB="{BB}"/>
                <DECO_AspectAngleCheck name="Gate2_AA_Lt90" UpDown="Less" AA="90" BB="{BB}"/>
                <DECO_DistanceCheck name="Gate2_Remerge_DistGt3000" UpDown="Greater" Distance="3000" BB="{BB}"/>
                <DECO_DistanceCheck name="Gate2_Remerge_DistLt5000" UpDown="Less" Distance="5000" BB="{BB}"/>
                <DECO_ClosureRateCheck name="Gate2_Closing" UpDown="Greater" Closure="0" BB="{BB}"/>
                <Task_LeadTurn name="Gate2_LeadTurn" BB="{BB}"/>
              </Sequence>

              <!-- Sustained close-range stalemate (neither side breaking off, both off-boresight):
                   Distance<2000m, both ATA>45 -> Scissors family. Nested Fallback, not a separate
                   top-level gate, since it's tactically what happens when a merge doesn't resolve
                   (plan §1) - the direct stalemate-breaker for the documented BT-vs-BT draw. -->
              <Sequence>
                <DECO_DistanceCheck name="Gate2_Neutral_DistLt2000" UpDown="Less" Distance="2000" BB="{BB}"/>
                <DECO_LOSCheck name="Gate2_Neutral_OwnATA_Gt45" UpDown="Greater" InputLOS="45" Which="Own" BB="{BB}"/>
                <DECO_LOSCheck name="Gate2_Neutral_TgtATA_Gt45" UpDown="Greater" InputLOS="45" Which="Target" BB="{BB}"/>
                <Fallback>
                  <Task_FlatScissors name="Gate2_FlatScissors" BB="{BB}"/>
                  <Task_VerticalScissors name="Gate2_VerticalScissors" BB="{BB}"/>
                  <Task_RollingScissors name="Gate2_RollingScissors" BB="{BB}"/>
                </Fallback>
              </Sequence>

            </Fallback>

            <!-- GATE 2.5 MOVED 2026-08-06: it now sits directly under Gate 0, above Gate 1.
                 See the block there for the measurement that motivated the move. -->

            <!-- GATE 3 - Energy-ratio guard, scoped narrowly in front of the offensive branches
                 only (scope decision: leave the existing safety/defensive priority order above
                 untouched). The "matched" band (now 0.8 to 1.4) falls through unchanged to Gate 4.
                 EDIT 2026-08-01 (hypothesis H1, energy-guard shadowing): superior threshold raised
                 1.2 to 1.4. At only a ~20 percent energy edge (1.2), Gate 3 committed to
                 Task_AnglesTactics (a flat pressing turn) and shadowed the entire Gate 4 selector,
                 including HighYoYoUp, the better answer when close and overtaking. Reserving the
                 commitment for a clearer ~40 percent edge routes marginal-advantage geometry to
                 Gate 4's situation-specific maneuvers instead. The inferior threshold (0.8) is left
                 ALONE on purpose: it gates Task_EnergyTactics (extend/survive when disadvantaged),
                 a survival behavior not worth weakening blind. UNTESTED on this box; revert by
                 changing 1.4 back to 1.2. Reading Task_Notch.cpp REFUTED the paired H3 (Notch
                 over-fire): Notch already self-gates narrowly (beam 70-110 deg, Distance under
                 2000 m, 1.5 s cap) and does not fire when we are the one behind, so it was left
                 untouched. -->
            <Fallback>
              <Sequence>
                <DECO_EnergyRatioCheck name="Gate3_Ratio_Gt1_4" UpDown="Greater" Ratio="1.4" BB="{BB}"/>
                <Task_AnglesTactics name="Gate3_AnglesTactics" BB="{BB}"/>
              </Sequence>
              <Sequence>
                <DECO_EnergyRatioCheck name="Gate3_Ratio_Lt0_8" UpDown="Less" Ratio="0.8" BB="{BB}"/>
                <Task_EnergyTactics name="Gate3_EnergyTactics" BB="{BB}"/>
              </Sequence>
            </Fallback>

            <!-- GATE 4 - Offensive maneuver selector. Bare Fallback children (plan §1): each
                 phased node self-gates its own distance/speed/altitude entry internally on a
                 fresh claim, same idiom as Gate 1. Task_OneCircleFight/Task_LagPursuit are the
                 original (pre-expansion) pursuit nodes, kept as the fallback within this gate. -->
            <Fallback>
              <Task_HighYoYoUp name="Gate4_HighYoYoUp" BB="{BB}"/>
              <Task_BarrelRollAttack name="Gate4_BarrelRollAttack" BB="{BB}"/>
              <Task_LowYoYo name="Gate4_LowYoYo" BB="{BB}"/>
              <Task_NoseToTailTurn name="Gate4_NoseToTailTurn" BB="{BB}"/>
              <Task_LagDisplacementRoll name="Gate4_LagDisplacementRoll" BB="{BB}"/>
              <Task_OneCircleFight name="Gate4_OneCircleFight" BB="{BB}"/>
              <Task_LagPursuit name="Gate4_LagPursuit" BB="{BB}"/>
            </Fallback>

            <!-- TAIL - final fallback pursuit nodes, from the original 8-node tree, plus the new
                 pre-filter. Task_SingleSideOffset is BARE (see file header note: a wrapping
                 Distance 3000-5000 Sequence would cut it off mid-maneuver; it self-gates instead). -->
            <Task_SingleSideOffset name="Tail_SingleSideOffset" BB="{BB}"/>

            <Sequence>
              <DECO_DistanceCheck name="Tail_DistGt2000" UpDown="Greater" Distance="2000" BB="{BB}"/>
              <Task_LeadPursuit name="Tail_LeadPursuit" BB="{BB}"/>
            </Sequence>

            <Sequence>
              <DECO_DistanceCheck name="Tail_DistLt2000" UpDown="Less" Distance="2000" BB="{BB}"/>
              <Task_Pure name="Tail_Pure" BB="{BB}"/>
            </Sequence>

          </Fallback>

        </Sequence>


    </Sequence>
  </BehaviorTree>


</root>
